            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: AI Copy Homogenization Is a "Man-Made" Problem, Not an Inevitable One</h2>
<p>The reason AI-generated ad copy is becoming increasingly uniform is not due to insufficient LLM capabilities, but because most marketers "feed" AI in the same way—using identical prompt templates, inputting similar materials, and expecting AI to "automatically" produce differentiated content. This is like giving 100 chefs the same ingredients and recipe and expecting completely different dishes.</p>
<h2>Three Core Problems Causing Homogenization</h2>
<h3>1. Template-Based Prompts: What You Feed Is Routine, So AI Outputs Routine</h3>
<p>Most AI ad copy prompts circulating online are generic templates: "Generate 5 Facebook ad copies, highlighting product features, with a call to action." When your competitors use the exact same prompts, AI answers naturally converge.</p>
<p><strong>Operational Insight</strong>: A tool app marketing team conducted a test—after using the same prompt to generate 50 copies and deduplicating them, similarity exceeded 70%.</p>
<h3>2. Single Source of Input: Even a Skilled Cook Can't Make a Meal Without Ingredients</h3>
<p>AI output quality directly depends on input material quality. If you only give AI a dry product specification sheet without real user feedback, competitive differentiation highlights, or scenario-based usage stories, AI can only "fill in blanks" based on generic knowledge, naturally lacking uniqueness.</p>
<h3>3. Ignoring Platform Characteristics: One Copy for All Platforms</h3>
<p>Facebook users and TikTok users have completely different reading habits, yet many teams distribute the same AI-generated copy across all platforms. AI doesn't understand platform nuances, so it can only deliver "average" answers.</p>
<h2>Four Breakthrough Methods</h2>
<h3>Method 1: Build a Private Prompt Library</h3>
<p>Don't rely on generic online templates—instead, build an exclusive prompt system tailored to your product, audience, and advertising platforms.</p>
<p><strong>Action Steps</strong>:<br />
1. Classify prompts by platform (Facebook/TikTok/Google)<br />
2. Subdivide templates by advertising objective (click/conversion/brand awareness)<br />
3. Record the best-performing prompt variants after each test<br />
4. Optimize and update the prompt library monthly</p>
<h3>Method 2: Forcefully Add "Differentiation Factors"</h3>
<p>Before AI generation, manually embed differentiation anchors: competitive advantages, genuine user testimonial fragments, holiday/trend connections. AI doesn't create differentiation—it amplifies it.</p>
<p><strong>Practical Tip</strong>: When inputting prompts, add a line: "Reference these user quotes: XXXX, highlight differences from competitor XX"—AI output will be noticeably more distinctive.</p>
<h3>Method 3: Human Intervention Is Essential</h3>
<p>AI generation produces drafts; human optimization produces finished work. Maintain a workflow of "AI initial screening + human revision," removing formulaic expressions (like "limited-time offer" or "don't miss out") and replacing them with more brand-aligned language.</p>
<h3>Method 4: Train AI on Your Own Copy</h3>
<p>Feed AI your top 10-20 historically best-performing ad copies as examples, letting it learn your brand voice and expression style. Output copy will naturally carry "family resemblance" and distinguish itself from competitors.</p>
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>AI is an amplifier, not magic. The clearer your differentiated positioning, the more recognizable your AI-generated copy becomes. Instead of complaining about AI homogenization, first examine whether your prompt design and material inputs are differentiated enough.</p>
